#Patches Denormal

Subnormal floats — values smaller in magnitude than the smallest normal
`f32` (~1.18e-38) — are a performance hazard in audio DSP.  A filter
with a feedback path can settle into subnormal territory during long
stretches of silence; on x86 each subnormal operation triggers a
microcode trap costing tens of cycles.  The audible effect is nil
(~-700 dBFS) but CPU rises during silence, exactly when headroom should
be greatest.

The fix is to flush subnormals to zero.  #flush_denormal returns its
argument unchanged when it is a normal float (or already zero), and zero
otherwise.  This is the scalar fallback; production audio code also sets
the hardware flush-to-zero bit per callback, but the scalar guard is
what the feedback kernels reach for inline.

    pub fn flush_denormal(x: f32) -> f32 {
        if !x.is_normal() && x != 0.0 {
            0.0
        } else {
            x
        }
    }

A normal value passes through untouched; a subnormal collapses to zero.

~example
    flush_denormal(1.0) == 1.0
    flush_denormal(0.0) == 0.0
    flush_denormal(-2.5) == -2.5
    flush_denormal(1.0e-40) == 0.0

##Closure under flushing

Whatever the input — normal, subnormal, zero, infinity, or NaN — the
result is always either exactly zero or a normal float.  The output set
is closed: feeding any output back through #flush_denormal leaves it
unchanged.

~property closed-under-flush
    proptest! {
        fn closed(x in proptest::num::f32::ANY) {
            let y = flush_denormal(x);
            prop_assert!(y == 0.0 || y.is_normal());
            prop_assert_eq!(flush_denormal(y), y);
        }
    }

---

#Tests

##normal values pass through
    flush_denormal(0.5) == 0.5
    flush_denormal(-1.0e30) == -1.0e30

##non-normal values flush to zero
    flush_denormal(1.0e-40) == 0.0
    flush_denormal(f32::MIN_POSITIVE / 2.0) == 0.0
